{
  "abstract": "The Legendre family of elliptic curves y^2 = x(x-1)(x-λ) has the period function F(λ) = 2F1(1/2, 1/2; 1; λ), taken here as Mathlib's ordinaryHypergeometric. Its Picard–Fuchs operator is λ(1-λ) y'' + (1-2λ) y' - y/4. The second Frobenius solution at λ = 0 is L(λ) = F(λ) log λ + G(λ), with G(λ) = sum over n of a_n h_n λ^n, a_n = (C(2n,n)/4^n)^2 the Taylor coefficients of F, and h_n = sum_{k=1}^{n} 2/(k(2k-1)). Let D be the slit unit disc, the open unit disc minus (-1, 0]. This entry proves, in Lean 4 and Mathlib, seven statements.\n(1) F is holomorphic on the unit disc and solves the Picard–Fuchs equation there. (2) Period integral: for |λ| < 1, F(λ) = (1/π) ∫_0^1 dx / (sqrt(x(1-x)) (1-λx)^{1/2}), with the principal branch of the complex power. (3) Wronskian: F L' - F' L = 1/(λ(1-λ)) on D. (4) Solution space: a function y is holomorphic on D and solves the Picard–Fuchs equation on D if and only if y = aF + bL on D for some complex constants a, b. (5) F and L are linearly independent on D. (6) Local monodromy at 0: there is a function holomorphic on the unit disc slit along [0, 1) that equals L on the open upper half-disc and L + 2πi F on the open lower half-disc; that is, L continues analytically across (-1, 0) to L + 2πi F, while F is holomorphic on the whole disc. In the basis (F, L), the local monodromy at 0 is therefore [[1, 2πi], [0, 1]]. (7) Connection formula: on the lens where |λ| < 1 and |λ - 1| < 1, F(1-λ) = (4 log 2 · F(λ) - L(λ))/π. This expresses the solution regular at λ = 1 in the Frobenius basis at λ = 0; its constants come from the classical asymptotic K(k) = log(4/k') + o(1) as k → 1, which is proved here from the period integral.\nThe proofs are a power-series treatment of the equation and of G (whose coefficients satisfy a harmonic-number recursion), Abel's identity for the Wronskian, a uniqueness theorem for holomorphic solutions with vanishing Cauchy data at an ordinary point (derivative recursion, Taylor series and the identity theorem; Mathlib's ODE uniqueness is for real time only), term-by-term integration of the binomial series against Beta integrals, and an explicit estimate of the complete elliptic integral near its logarithmic singularity. All seven results are classical; this is, to our knowledge, their first Lean formalization. Scope: everything is local to λ = 0 (and, for (7), to the lens); the global monodromy group Γ(2) and analytic continuation along paths are not formalized; the elliptic curve, its differential forms and its homology do not appear, so \"period\" refers to the integral formula (2).",
